## Test Smell Audit — `CatalogTest.java`

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 4           | 4              |
| Medium   | 4           | 4              |
| Low      | 2           | 2              |
| **Total**| **10**      | **10**         |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### 🔴 HIGH

**1. Conditional Test Logic** — `addConditionalLogic()` (line 26)

The `if (sku.equals("b"))` branch inside the loop means each SKU follows a different path. If the `"b"` branch breaks, the single aggregate assertion may still pass by accident.

```java
// Current (smell)
for (String sku : skus) {
    if (sku.equals("b")) { catalog.add(sku, 10); }
    else                 { catalog.add(sku, 1);  }
}
assertEquals(12, catalog.quantity("a") + catalog.quantity("b") + catalog.quantity("c"));
```

**Fix:** Replace with explicit, straight-line calls — no branching:

```java
@Test void addMultipleSkus() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    catalog.add("b", 10);
    catalog.add("c", 1);
    assertEquals(1,  catalog.quantity("a"));
    assertEquals(10, catalog.quantity("b"));
    assertEquals(1,  catalog.quantity("c"));
}
```

---

**2. Mystery Guest** — `addLoadsFromFile()` (line 40)

The test creates, writes, and reads a temp file on disk. Any I/O failure causes a flaky result that has nothing to do with `Catalog`.

```java
Path tmp = Files.createTempFile("catalog", ".txt");
Files.writeString(tmp, "x:7");
String[] parts = Files.readString(tmp).split(":");
```

**Fix:** Inline the data — no filesystem needed:

```java
@Test void addFromParsedInput() {
    String input = "x:7";
    String[] parts = input.split(":");
    catalog.add(parts[0], Integer.parseInt(parts[1]));
    assertTrue(catalog.inStock("x"));
}
```

---

**3. Sleepy Test** — `addSleepy()` (line 50)

`Thread.sleep(200)` adds 200 ms with zero purpose — `Catalog` has no async behavior.

```java
Thread.sleep(200);
```

**Fix:** Delete the sleep entirely:

```java
@Test void addSingleItem() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

---

**4. Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)** — `addAndDoNothing()` (line 58)

The test calls `catalog.add("a", 1)` then ends — no assertion at all. It will pass even if `add()` silently discards the item.

```java
@Test void addAndDoNothing() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);  // no assertion
}
```

**Fix:** Assert the expected post-condition:

```java
@Test void addSingleItem_inStock() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertTrue(catalog.inStock("a"));
}
```

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM

**5. Eager Test** — `doEverythingAtOnce()` (line 64)

Exercises 4 distinct production methods (`add`, `quantity`, `inStock`, `toString`) in one test. A failure gives no signal about which method is broken.

```java
catalog.add("a", 1); catalog.add("b", 2); catalog.add("c", 3);
catalog.quantity("a"); catalog.quantity("b");
catalog.inStock("c");
catalog.toString();
```

**Fix:** Split into focused tests — one behavior per test:

```java
@Test void quantityReflectsAdds() {
    catalog.add("a", 1);
    assertEquals(1, catalog.quantity("a"));
}

@Test void inStockReturnsTrueAfterAdd() {
    catalog.add("c", 3);
    assertTrue(catalog.inStock("c"));
}
```

---

**6. Magic Number Test** — `quantityAfterAdds()` (line 77)

41 and 42 have no contextual meaning. A reader must mentally compute 41 + 1 to verify correctness.

```java
catalog.add("a", 41);
catalog.add("a", 1);
assertEquals(42, catalog.quantity("a"));
```

**Fix:** Use clearly-derived values:

```java
@Test void addMergesQuantityForSameSku() {
    int first = 5, second = 3;
    catalog.add("a", first);
    catalog.add("a", second);
    assertEquals(first + second, catalog.quantity("a"));
}
```

